ng word","text":"A familiar place whose word once had a surprising connection to free time.","notice":"Compare the word forms, their meanings and their dates. What connection does the evidence support?","sourceIds":["etymology"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"How far back can we follow its name?"},{"title":"What the history tells us","text":"A familiar place whose word once had a surprising connection to free time."}]}},{"id":"school-1","form":"scol","languageId":"old-english","eraId":"medieval","placeId":"england","offset":[-12,-70],"period":"Before 900","meaning":"An older English learning word.","pronunciation":{"text":"scol","languageId":"old-english"},"evidence":{"kind":"manuscript","title":"An older English learning word","text":"An older English word list gives scol for a place of learning. It is an earlier form in the story of school. Its source sends us to Latin.","notice":"Compare scol with school. Which letters can you recognise? A spelling comparison alone does not tell us exactly how everyone pronounced it.","sourceIds":["etymology"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Compare scol with school. Which letters stayed?"},{"title":"What the history tells us","text":"An older English word list gives scol for a place of learning. It is an earlier form in the story of school. Its source sends us to Latin."}]}},{"id":"school-2","form":"schola","languageId":"latin","eraId":"roman","placeId":"rome","offset":[0,0],"period":"A place of learning","meaning":"A place of learning, in Latin.","pronunciation":{"text":"schola","languageId":"latin"},"evidence":{"kind":"tablet","title":"A Latin learning place","text":"Latin schola meant a place of instruction or a learned discussion. English scol came from Latin. But Latin had borrowed this word too.","notice":"Compare schola, scol and school. What remains recognisable as the word moves through languages?","sourceIds":["etymology"],"illustrative":true,"p