el","text":"A weekday name can hide an old story.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Can you hear a name inside Thursday?"},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"A weekday name can hide an old story."}]}},{"id":"thursday-old","form":"þunres dæg","languageId":"old-english","eraId":"medieval","placeId":"england","offset":[-18,-78],"period":"Before 1100","meaning":"Thunor’s day, in Old English.","pronunciation":{"text":"þunres dæg","languageId":"old-english"},"evidence":{"kind":"manuscript","title":"An English calendar clue","text":"Thunor was a thunder god. This older English name developed into Thursday, with influence from a Norse name too.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"The letter þ sounds like th. Dæg means day."},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"Thunor was a thunder god. This older English name developed into Thursday, with influence from a Norse name too."}]}},{"id":"thursday-norse","form":"þórsdagr","languageId":"old-norse","eraId":"medieval","placeId":"norse","offset":[0,0],"period":"Medieval Norse","meaning":"Thor’s day, in Old Norse.","pronunciation":{"text":"þórsdagr","languageId":"old-norse"},"evidence":{"kind":"manuscript","title":"A northern calendar clue","text":"The Norse name helped shape the English one. Thor and Thunor belong to the same thunder-god tradition.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Þórr is Thor. Compare the first part with Thurs."},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"The Norse name helped shape the English one. Thor and Thunor belong to the same thunder-god tradition."}]}},{"id":"thursday-german","form":"Donnerstag","languageId":"german","eraId":"today","placeId":"germany","offset":[0,0],"period":"Today","meaning":"Thursday, in German.","pronunciation":{"text":"Donnerstag","languageId":"german"},"evidence":{"kind":"card","title":"A German calendar clue","text":"Donnerstag also preserves a thunder-god day. It is a related naming tradition, rather than the source of the English word.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history","german"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Donner means thunder. Could different sounds hide a shared idea?"},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"Donnerstag also preserves a thunder-god day. It is a related naming tradition, rather than the source of the English word."}]}},{"id":"thursday-latin","form":"Iovis dies","languageId":"latin","eraId":"roman","placeId":"rome","offset":[0,0],"period":"Roman naming tradition","meaning":"Jupiter’s day, in Latin.","pronunciation":{"text":"Iovis dies","languageId":"latin"},"evidence":{"kind":"tablet","title":"A Roman calendar clue","text":"The Germanic name translated a Roman idea: a day for Jupiter became a day for a local thunder god. The idea travelled, rather than the Latin sounds.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Jupiter and Thunor were both linked with thunder."},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"The Germanic name translated a Roman idea: a day for Jupiter became a day for a local thunder god. The idea travelled, rather than the Latin sounds."}]}},{"id":"thursday-thirsty","form":"thirsty","languageId":"english","eraId":"today","placeId":"england","offset":[-90,20],"period":"Today","meaning":"Wanting a drink.","pronunciation":{"text":"thirsty","languageId":"english"},"evidence":{"kind":"card","title":"A similar-sounding word","text":"Thirsty sounds a little like Thursday, but Thursday’s history points to a thunder god, not a drink.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"A matching sound is not enough. Does the meaning fit?"},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"Thirsty sounds a little like Thursday, but Thursday’s history points to a thunder god, not a drink."}]}},{"id":"thursday-soundalike","form":"Durst","languageId":"german","eraId":"today","placeId":"germany","offset":[20,-42],"period":"Today","meaning":"Thirst, in German.","pronunciation":{"text":"Durst","languageId":"german"},"evidence":{"kind":"card","title":"A word to compare","text":"Durst means thirst. Thursday’s history is about a thunder god, not needing a drink. Check the older calendar names as well as the sounds.","notice":"Compare its sound and meaning, then check the word history.","sourceIds":["history","soundalike"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Listen and compare","text":"Say Durst and Thursday slowly. Similar sounds can start a theory. Does the meaning help too?"},{"title":"Check the history","text":"Durst means thirst. Thursday’s history is about a thunder god, not needing a drink. Check the older calendar names as well as the sounds."}]}}],"edges":[{"id":"thursday-now-to-thursday-old","source":"thursday-now","target":"thursday-old","type":"inheritance","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Yes: this is the thunder-god name behind Thursday.","sourceIds":["history"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Could this be an older English name for Thursday?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes: this is the thunder-god name behind Thursday."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":false,"feedback":"There is a connection to discover. Yes: this is the thunder-god name behind Thursday."}]}},{"id":"thursday-now-to-thursday-german","source":"thursday-now","target":"thursday-german","type":"related","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Yes. The names share an old naming tradition. English did not borrow Thursday from modern German.","sourceIds":["history","german"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Could this German name share Thursday’s thunder-god story?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes. The names share an old naming tradition. English did not borrow Thursday from modern German."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":false,"feedback":"There is a connection to discover. Yes. The names share an old naming tradition. English did not borrow Thursday from modern German."}]}},{"id":"thursday-now-to-thursday-thirsty","source":"thursday-now","target":"thursday-thirsty","type":"comparison","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Thursday belongs to the thunder-god story, not thirsty.","sourceIds":["history"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Did Thursday get its name from wanting a drink?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":false,"feedback":"Try comparing the dates and meanings again. Thursday belongs to the thunder-god story, not thirsty."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Thursday belongs to the thunder-god story, not thirsty."}]}},{"id":"thursday-old-to-thursday-norse","source":"thursday-old","target":"thursday-norse","type":"related","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Yes. Norse influence helped shape the English form.","sourceIds":["history"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Did this Norse name help shape the English name?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes. Norse influence helped shape the English form."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":false,"feedback":"There is a connection to discover. Yes. Norse influence helped shape the English form."}]}},{"id":"thursday-old-to-thursday-latin","source":"thursday-old","target":"thursday-latin","type":"translation","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Yes: Jupiter’s day was translated using a local thunder god. This is a travelling idea, not a borrowed Latin word.","sourceIds":["history"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Could a Roman naming idea be behind this English name?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes: Jupiter’s day was translated using a local thunder god. This is a travelling idea, not a borrowed Latin word."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":false,"feedback":"There is a connection to discover. Yes: Jupiter’s day was translated using a local thunder god. This is a travelling idea, not a borrowed Latin word."}]}},{"id":"thursday-soundalike-lead","source":"thursday-now","target":"thursday-soundalike","type":"comparison","evidence":{"title":"Test this word history","text":"Durst means thirst. Thursday’s history is about a thunder god, not needing a drink. Check the older calendar names as well as the sounds.","sourceIds":["history","soundalike"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Was Thursday named after German Durst, meaning thirst?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":false,"feedback":"A good theory to test. Durst means thirst. Thursday’s history is about a thunder god, not needing a drink. Check the older calendar names as well as the sounds."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Good catch! Durst means thirst. Thursday’s history is about a thunder god, not needing a drink. Check the older calendar names as well as the sounds."}]}}],"puzzles":[],"milestones":[{"nodeId":"thursday-now","label":"Today"},{"nodeId":"thursday-old","label":"Older English clue"},{"nodeId":"thursday-latin","label":"Roman naming idea"}],"$schema":"../case.schema.json"},{"schemaVersion":1,"id":"january","title":"January","icon":"📅","summary":"A new year, an old name, and a trail across the Channel.","question":"How did January get its name?","startNodeId":"january-now","goalNodeIds":["january-middle","january-french-old","january-latin"],"completion":"January’s older English forms came through French from Latin Ianuarius. Italian gennaio is another branch of that family.","partner":"A similar sound is a clue. Let’s check the evidence.","sources":[{"id":"history","title":"American Heritage · January","url":"https://www.ahdictionary.com/word/search.html?q=January"},{"id":"spelling","title":"Etymonline · January","url":"https://www.etymonline.com/word/January"},{"id":"italian","title":"Treccani · gennaio","url":"https://www.treccani.it/vocabolario/gennaio/"},{"id":"june","title":"Académie française · juin","url":"https://www.dictionnaire-academie.fr/article/A9J0358"},{"id":"janus","title":"Etymonline · Janus","url":"https://www.etymonline.com/word/Janus"},{"id":"soundalike","title":"CNRTL · genou","url":"https://www.cnrtl.fr/definition/genou"}],"nodes":[{"id":"january-now","form":"January","languageId":"english","eraId":"today","placeId":"england","offset":[0,0],"period":"Today","meaning":"The first month of the year.","pronunciation":{"text":"January","languageId":"english"},"evidence":{"kind":"label","title":"A calendar label","text":"The year opens with January. Its name has travelled through several languages.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Which other month names sound similar?"},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"The year opens with January. Its name has travelled through several languages."}]}},{"id":"january-middle","form":"Jenever","languageId":"middle-english","eraId":"medieval","placeId":"england","offset":[-18,-78],"period":"Medieval English","meaning":"A month name. Does its spelling look familiar?","pronunciation":{"text":"Jenever","languageId":"middle-english"},"evidence":{"kind":"manuscript","title":"A word on an old calendar","text":"Jenever is an older English form. Later English spelling moved closer to its Latin ancestor.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history","spelling"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Try saying Jenever and January slowly. Which sounds seem alike?"},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"Jenever is an older English form. Later English spelling moved closer to its Latin ancestor."}]}},{"id":"january-french-old","form":"Jenever","languageId":"old-french","eraId":"medieval","placeId":"france","offset":[0,0],"period":"Old North French","meaning":"January, in an older French form.","pronunciation":{"text":"Jenever","languageId":"old-french"},"evidence":{"kind":"manuscript","title":"A French calendar clue","text":"The older English month name came through Old North French. The matching Jenever forms are evidence of that borrowing.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Same spelling, different language: check the history."},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"The older English month name came through Old North French. The matching Jenever forms are evidence of that borrowing."}]}},{"id":"january-latin","form":"Ianuarius","languageId":"latin","eraId":"roman","placeId":"rome","offset":[0,0],"period":"Roman month name","meaning":"Janus’s month. Janus was the Roman god of doors and beginnings.","pronunciation":{"text":"Ianuarius","languageId":"latin"},"evidence":{"kind":"tablet","title":"A Roman calendar clue","text":"Ianuarius named the month for Janus, the Roman god of doors, gateways and beginnings. He was pictured with two faces looking in opposite directions. Imagine January as a doorway into a new year. This Latin name lies behind the English and French forms.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history","janus"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"I and J were not always written as separate letters."},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"Janus was the Roman god of doors and beginnings, pictured with two faces. Imagine one looking back at the old year and one ahead to the new: January is our doorway into a new year."}]}},{"id":"january-italian","form":"gennaio","languageId":"italian","eraId":"today","placeId":"florence","offset":[-18,-15],"period":"Today","meaning":"January, in Italian.","pronunciation":{"text":"gennaio","languageId":"italian"},"evidence":{"kind":"letter","title":"An Italian calendar clue","text":"Italian gennaio shares the Latin month-name ancestor. It is a cousin, not the word English borrowed directly.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["italian"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Try saying gennaio and January aloud."},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"Italian gennaio shares the Latin month-name ancestor. It is a cousin, not the word English borrowed directly."}]}},{"id":"january-june","form":"juin","languageId":"french","eraId":"today","placeId":"france","offset":[-30,0],"period":"Today","meaning":"June, in French.","pronunciation":{"text":"juin","languageId":"french"},"evidence":{"kind":"card","title":"Another calendar clue","text":"Juin names June, not January. The opening sound can tempt us onto the wrong trail.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["june"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Check which month this word names."},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"Juin names June, not January. The opening sound can tempt us onto the wrong trail."}]},"mapLabelSide":"left"},{"id":"january-soundalike","form":"genou","languageId":"french","eraId":"today","placeId":"france","offset":[0,40],"period":"Today","meaning":"A knee, in French.","pronunciation":{"text":"genou","languageId":"french"},"evidence":{"kind":"card","title":"A word to compare","text":"Genou means knee. January’s month-name trail leads through older French to Latin Ianuarius. A similar sound does not turn a body part into this month’s name.","notice":"Compare its sound and meaning, then check the word history.","sourceIds":["history","soundalike"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Listen and compare","text":"Say genou and January slowly. Similar sounds can start a theory. Does the meaning help too?"},{"title":"Check the history","text":"Genou means knee. January’s month-name trail leads through older French to Latin Ianuarius. A similar sound does not turn a body part into this month’s name."}]}}],"edges":[{"id":"january-now-to-january-middle","source":"january-now","target":"january-middle","type":"spelling","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Yes: Jenever is an older English form of the month name.","sourceIds":["history"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Could Jenever be connected to our word January?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes: Jenever is an older English form of the month name."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":false,"feedback":"There is a connection to discover. Yes: Jenever is an older English form of the month name."}]}},{"id":"january-now-to-january-italian","source":"january-now","target":"january-italian","type":"related","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Yes. Italian and English share a Latin ancestor here.","sourceIds":["history","italian"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Could gennaio and January have an older month name in common?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes. Italian and English share a Latin ancestor here."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":false,"feedback":"There is a connection to discover. Yes. Italian and English share a Latin ancestor here."}]}},{"id":"january-now-to-january-june","source":"january-now","target":"january-june","type":"comparison","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"No: this clue names June, a different month.","sourceIds":["june"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Could this French word for June explain the name January?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":false,"feedback":"Try comparing the dates and meanings again. No: this clue names June, a different month."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":true,"feedback":"No: this clue names June, a different month."}]}},{"id":"january-middle-to-january-french-old","source":"january-middle","target":"january-french-old","type":"borrowing","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Yes. The earlier English form came through Old North French.","sourceIds":["history"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Could English have borrowed this older French month name?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes. The earlier English form came through Old North French."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":false,"feedback":"There is a connection to discover. Yes. The earlier English form came through Old North French."}]}},{"id":"january-french-old-to-january-latin","source":"january-french-old","target":"january-latin","type":"inheritance","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Yes. The French name developed from the Latin name for Janus’s month.","sourceIds":["history"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Could this Latin month name be the older ancestor?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes. The French name developed from the Latin name for Janus’s month."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":false,"feedback":"There is a connection to discover. Yes. The French name developed from the Latin name for Janus’s month."}]}},{"id":"january-italian-to-january-latin","source":"january-italian","target":"january-latin","type":"inheritance","evidence":{"title":"Test this connection","text":"Yes. Gennaio and January meet at a Latin month name.","sourceIds":["history","italian"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Could this be the Latin ancestor of gennaio?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes. Gennaio and January meet at a Latin month name."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":false,"feedback":"There is a connection to discover. Yes. Gennaio and January meet at a Latin month name."}]}},{"id":"january-soundalike-lead","source":"january-now","target":"january-soundalike","type":"comparison","evidence":{"title":"Test this word history","text":"Genou means knee. January’s month-name trail leads through older French to Latin Ianuarius. A similar sound does not turn a body part into this month’s name.","sourceIds":["history","soundalike"]},"challenge":{"prompt":"Did the month name January come from French genou?","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, I think so","correct":false,"feedback":"A good theory to test. Genou means knee. January’s month-name trail leads through older French to Latin Ianuarius. A similar sound does not turn a body part into this month’s name."},{"id":"no","label":"No, I don’t think so","correct":true,"feedback":"Good catch! Genou means knee. January’s month-name trail leads through older French to Latin Ianuarius. A similar sound does not turn a body part into this month’s name."}]}}],"puzzles":[],"milestones":[{"nodeId":"january-now","label":"Today"},{"nodeId":"january-middle","label":"Older English clue"},{"nodeId":"january-french-old","label":"French clue"},{"nodeId":"january-latin","label":"Latin month name"}],"$schema":"../case.schema.json"},{"schemaVersion":1,"id":"robot","title":"robot","icon":"🤖","summary":"A word that escaped from a theatre and travelled the world.","question":"Where was the word robot invented?","startNodeId":"robot-now","goalNodeIds":["robot-czech","robot-work"],"completion":"English borrowed robot from Czech. The 1920 play R.U.R. used a name suggested by Josef Čapek, from robota. German later borrowed its machine word from English.","partner":"A similar sound is a clue. Let’s check the evidence.","sources":[{"id":"history","title":"Czech Embassy · 100 years of robot","url":"https://mzv.gov.cz/kualalumpur/en/news_and_events/archive/x100_year_ago_robot_was_born.html"},{"id":"german","title":"Duden · Roboter","url":"https://www.duden.de/rechtschreibung/Roboter"},{"id":"soundalike","title":"Académie française · rabot","url":"https://www.dictionnaire-academie.fr/article/A9R0043"}],"nodes":[{"id":"robot-now","form":"robot","languageId":"english","eraId":"today","placeId":"england","offset":[0,0],"period":"Today","meaning":"A machine that can carry out tasks.","pronunciation":{"text":"robot","languageId":"english"},"evidence":{"kind":"label","title":"A robot label","text":"This word travelled out of a play and into everyday speech.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Could a word for a machine begin in a theatre?"},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"This word travelled out of a play and into everyday speech."}]}},{"id":"robot-czech","form":"robot","languageId":"czech","eraId":"twentieth","placeId":"prague","offset":[-30,7],"period":"R.U.R. · published 1920","meaning":"Robot: a new name for a made-up worker.","pronunciation":{"text":"robot","languageId":"czech"},"evidence":{"kind":"script","title":"A theatre script","text":"Karel Čapek used robot in R.U.R. Josef, his brother, suggested the name. The play helped send the word into other languages.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"This is the new name used in a play in 1920. An older Czech word helped make it."},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"Karel Čapek used robot in R.U.R. Josef, his brother, suggested the name. The play helped send the word into other languages."}]},"mapLabelSide":"left","mapCaption":"New name · 1920"},{"id":"robot-work","form":"robota","languageId":"czech","eraId":"twentieth","placeId":"prague","offset":[20,12],"period":"Older Czech word used to form the 1920 name","meaning":"Robota: the older Czech word for forced work.","pronunciation":{"text":"robota","languageId":"czech"},"evidence":{"kind":"card","title":"A Czech word clue","text":"Still in the Czech lands! Robota meant forced work. Josef Čapek used this older word to suggest robot, the new name in his brother’s play.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["history"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Take away the final a. What remains?"},{"title":"A little more evidence","text":"Still in the Czech lands! Robota meant forced work. Josef Čapek used this older word to suggest robot, the new name in his brother’s play."}]},"mapCaption":"Older word · forced work"},{"id":"robot-german","form":"Roboter","languageId":"german","eraId":"today","placeId":"germany","offset":[-18,-50],"period":"Today","meaning":"Robot, in German.","pronunciation":{"text":"Roboter","languageId":"german"},"evidence":{"kind":"card","title":"A German word clue","text":"German Roboter took its machine meaning from English robot. This trail shows our English word travelling outwards.","notice":"Compare the sounds, meaning and dates. Similar words need historical evidence too.","sourceIds":["german"],"illustrative":true,"pages":[{"title":"Look a little closer","text":"Dates and borrowing direction matter: which